\par\pard\plain\s8\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\sl240\slmult1\sb119\f1\fs28\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 FLLD\expnd-2\expndtw-8  \expnd0\expndtw0 (Front\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lateral\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 Load\expnd-2\expndtw-8  \expnd0\expndtw-2 Distribution)\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\ri437\sl244\slmult1\sb122\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 When a car makes a turn, weight is transferred from the inside tires to the outside tires.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, how this weight transfer is split between the front and rear has a huge impact on the feel and handling of the car.\expnd13\expndtw63  \expnd0\expndtw0 If more weight transfer occurs on the front of the car, the outside front tire is being \'93overworked\'94 more than the rear outside tire, causing less cornering traction at the front.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This is more\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 likely\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 to produce\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 understeer\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 or\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 push.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Very\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 simplistically,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 if\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Front\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lateral\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 Load\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Distribution\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 50%,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 that\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 means\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 weight transfer split between front and rear is the same, and that should produce neutral handling.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The book \'93Race Car Vehicle Dynamics\'94 by Milliken and Milliken (with assistance by Terry Satchell) discusses this concept in detail.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This concept is similar to the idea of balancing the front and rear roll angles presented in recent magazine articles.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\ri465\sl244\slmult1\sb116\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Figure A7 shows the program displaying the FLLD for the current vehicle (currently 43.4%, which would tend to oversteer) during the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 transition between releasing the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 brakes\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 and going to the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 throttle,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 at\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 apex of\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 turn (transistion between braking and\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 power). During braking and under acceleration, the weight transfer is much more complicated, and the FLLD concept can not be as easily \expnd0\expndtw-2 applied.\par
