\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\ri374\sl244\slmult1\sb132\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Figure A8 is displayed if you click on the \'93Find\'94 button shown in Figure A7.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This \'93Find FLLD\'94 screen lets you find a certain Front Lateral Load Distribution (FLLD), which can\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 be a good indication of how the car will handle at the apex or transistion of the turn (no power, no braking).\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb6\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\ri465\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Pick the 'Adjust' factor to tell the program what vehicle component(s) you want to adjust.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Enter your desired 'For This FLLD', then click on the 'Find Now' button.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For perfect theoretical 'Balance', the FLLD should be 50%.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, from experience, Milliken suggests a target\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 'starting point'\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 FLLD value of 5 percentage points higher than the percent\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 weight\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 on the front\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 tires (100% -\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Rear Wt\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\ri465\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 %\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 =\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Front\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 Wt\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 %).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 By\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 default,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 program\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 will\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 load in this\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 value,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 but\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 you can change\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 it\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 to anything else\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 you want.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Higher\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 FLLDs tend to make the car tighter, with more understeer.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lower FLLDs tend to make the car looser, with more oversteer.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li720\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 There\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 are\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 several ways\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 to obtain\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 a\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 certain Front\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lateral\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Load\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Distrbution \expnd0\expndtw-2 percentage.\par
